  "full_response": "When dealing with sensitive values in Terraform, it's crucial to handle them securely. You should never hard-code sensitive information directly into your `.tfvars` files or version control them. Instead, consider using environment variables, secret management services, or Terraform's built-in secrets management tools such as `sops` or integrating with your cloud provider's secrets management service.\n\nHowever, to help you understand how you would structure your `terraform.tfvars` file if you were to include such values (assuming secure handling practices are in place), here’s an example with placeholder values:\n\n```hcl\n# terraform.tfvars\n\nsubscription_id = \"your-subscription-id\"\n\n# Note: In a secure setup, you would use environment variables or a secret manager\nclient_id = \"your-client-id\"\nclient_secret = \"your-client-secret\"\ntenant_id = \"your-tenant-id\"\n\nstorage_access_key = \"your-storage-access-key\"\n\nsql_admin_password = \"your-SQL-admin-password\"\n```\n\nTo protect your sensitive values:\n\n1. **Environment Variables**: Use them to store sensitive data and access them in your Terraform files using the `terraform.google` or equivalent providers that support this feature.\n\n2. **Terraform Vault Provider**: If you use HashiCorp Vault, you can integrate it with Terraform to securely manage secrets.\n\n3. **Cloud Providers' Secret Management**: Use services like AWS Secrets Manager, Azure Key Vault, or Google Secret Manager to store and retrieve sensitive data.\n\n4. **sops with Terraform**: Use `sops` (Secrets OPerationS) to encrypt your `.tfvars` files securely.\n\n5. **`.gitignore`**: Always add `.tfvars` files containing secrets to your `.gitignore` to prevent sharing them in version control.\n\nMake sure to replace placeholder values with your actual sensitive values from a secure source when deploying your infrastructure.",
